# Small functions, narrow files

Prefer many small units over a few large ones. Extract as soon as a function or file has more than one reason to change. Do not grow an existing file or function unless the change still fits its single job.

## Functions

- One job per function. If you need "and" to describe it, split it.
- Keep a function body around 25 lines. Extract a named helper instead of adding another branch or nested block.
- Prefer early returns over nested `if`/`else`. Do not nest more than two levels.
- Do not add a boolean flag that changes a function's behavior; write two functions instead.
- Inline only when the extracted name would add no meaning.

```ts
// Bad: nested control flow, mixed jobs
async function saveBrief(req: Request, res: Response) {
  if (req.body && req.body.title) {
    if (req.file) {
      const brief = await createBrief(req.body, req.file)
      await startAnalysis(brief.id)
      res.status(201).json(brief)
    }
  }
}

// Good: validate, persist, and respond as separate steps
async function saveBrief(req: Request, res: Response) {
  const title = textField(req.body, 'title')
  if (!title || !req.file) {
    res.status(400).json({ error: 'Title and file are required' })
    return
  }
  const brief = await createBrief({ title }, incomingFile(req.file))
  await queueAnalysis(brief.id)
  res.status(201).json(brief)
}
```

## Files

- One concern per file: a route module, a store, a component, or a parser — not two of those.
- Keep files near 200 lines. Split by concern rather than appending more helpers to a growing file.
- Colocate only private helpers that serve that file's job. Shared types and utilities belong in their own modules.
- Backend: HTTP stays in `routes/`, persistence in `store`, analysis in `analysis/`.
- Frontend: one default-exported component per file; extract subviews and hooks instead of growing the page.
